// Sections 6-15: lexical structure.

render_section_6 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A source file is a sequence of bytes that the compiler reads as UTF-8
        \\text, and nothing else is accepted. A root file whose bytes are not
        \\valid UTF-8 is rejected before lexing begins, with `glosso: cannot
        \\read` followed by the file name; a file reached through `#load` fails
        \\with `could not read`, the path, and `stream did not contain valid
        \\UTF-8`. An encoding mistake is therefore always a read failure, never a
        \\stray token halfway down the file.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Past that point the scanner works on decoded code points rather than
        \\bytes. The line, column and length recorded on every token are counted
        \\in code points from the start of the line, which is what keeps a caret
        \\span the same width as the token it underlines when the line holds
        \\non-Latin text.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\main :: () {
        \\    안녕 := 1;
        \\    λμν := 안녕 + oops;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\That program fails with `error: unbound 'oops'`, reported at `3:17`.
        \\Column 17 counts the sixteen code points before `oops`; a byte count
        \\would have said 24, because `λ`, `μ` and `ν` occupy two bytes each and
        \\`안` and `녕` occupy three.
    );
    subheading(4, "Whitespace");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Exactly four code points are skipped as whitespace. The scanner tests
        \\for them before anything else, and only for them.
    );
    table_header(6, "Code point   —   How the scanner treats it");
    table_row(7, "U+000A LF   |   Ends the line and advances the line counter.");
    table_row(8, "U+000D CR   |   Skipped, which is why a CRLF file lexes exactly like an LF file.");
    table_row(9, "U+0020 SP   |   Skipped.");
    table_row(10, "U+0009 HT   |   Skipped, and counted as a single column.");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\Nothing else is whitespace. Any other code point falls through to token
        \\dispatch, and one that starts no token becomes a stray-character error
        \\whose entire message is the offending character in single quotes. Three
        \\invisible characters reach that path often enough to be worth naming.
    );
    table_header(12, "Character   —   Diagnostic   —   Usual source");
    table_row(13, "U+FEFF   |   `error: ''`, with the byte-order mark itself between the quotes, so nothing is visible   |   Saving as UTF-8 with BOM");
    table_row(14, "U+000C   |   `error: ''`, likewise empty-looking   |   A form feed used as a page break");
    table_row(15, "U+00A0   |   `error: ' '`, which looks exactly like a space   |   Pasting from a web page");
    note(16, #string,oneline
        \\A byte-order mark sits at 1:1, so a BOM-prefixed file does not compile
        \\at all: the first thing the scanner sees is a character it cannot use.
        \\An editor that offers both `UTF-8 with BOM` and `UTF-8` produces a
        \\working file only with the second.
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Because a carriage return is whitespace, line endings do not matter to
        \\the scanner. The one place the distinction survives is inside a
        \\multi-line block, where the carriage return is part of the literal
        \\text; that case is covered in `#string` Multi-Line Blocks.
    );
    subheading(18, "Line comments");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`//` begins a comment that runs to the next newline, or to the end of
        \\input if there is none. Everything after it on that line is invisible
        \\to the scanner, including quotes, `/*` and `*/`.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", 10 // 2 is never divided
        \\    );
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\10
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Comments are recognised before token dispatch, so a `/` immediately
        \\followed by `/` or by `*` can never begin an operator. A library may
        \\declare an operator named with those characters, but no use site can
        \\ever spell it; see Reserved Operators and Operator Names.
    );
    subheading(23, "Block comments");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`/*` opens a comment that `*/` closes, and the pair nests. The scanner
        \\keeps a depth counter rather than searching for the first `*/`, so a
        \\region that already contains comments can be commented out as a whole.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\/* outer
        \\   /* inner nested comment */
        \\   still inside the outer comment
        \\*/
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    x := 1 /* inline */ + /* /* nested */ */ 2;
        \\    print("x = %\n", x);
        \\    print("[%]\n", "http://example.com /* not a comment */");
        \\}
        \\/* an unterminated block comment at the end of the file
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\x = 3
        \\[http://example.com /* not a comment */]
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Two further facts are visible there. A newline inside a block comment
        \\still advances the line counter, so diagnostics after a large
        \\commented-out region report the right line. And an unterminated `/*` at
        \\the end of the file is accepted silently: the scanner runs out of input
        \\and emits the end-of-input token. There is no unterminated-comment
        \\diagnostic.
    );
    note(28, #string,oneline
        \\The depth counter reacts to `/*` and `*/` everywhere inside the
        \\comment, including inside text that looks like a string literal. A
        \\comment is scanned as characters, not as tokens, so nothing protects a
        \\`*/` written between quotes.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\main :: () {
        \\    /* the pattern "*/" ends this comment early */
        \\    x := 1;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The comment ends at the `*/` inside the quotes. What remains of the
        \\line opens a string literal that no quote closes, and the file fails
        \\with `error: unterminated string literal`, underlining from that quote
        \\to the end of the line.
    );
    bullet(31, #string,oneline
        \\A `/*` written inside a `//` comment opens nothing; the line comment
        \\already owns the rest of the line.
    );
    bullet(32, #string,oneline
        \\A `//` written inside a block comment does not close it, and neither
        \\does a newline.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\A `/*` or `*/` written inside a string literal is ordinary text: the
        \\scanner looks for comments only where a token could start.
    );
}

render_section_7 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An identifier begins with `_` or an alphabetic code point, and
        \\continues with `_` or an alphanumeric one. There is no length limit and
        \\no restriction to ASCII.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The two classes come from Unicode tables generated into the compiler
        \\and built to match CPython's single-character `str.isalpha` and
        \\`str.isalnum`. Carrying the tables rather than borrowing the host
        \\language's own predicates means the accepted identifier set does not
        \\shift when the toolchain that built the compiler updates its Unicode
        \\version.
    );
    table_header(2, "Position   —   Class   —   Also accepted");
    table_row(3, "First character   |   Alphabetic, `isalpha`   |   `_`");
    table_row(4, "Later characters   |   Alphanumeric, `isalnum`   |   `_`");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\`isalnum` is the union of the alphabetic, decimal, digit and numeric
        \\classes, so it is strictly wider than `isalpha`. Some code points can
        \\therefore continue an identifier without being able to start one.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    λ := 1;
        \\    안녕 := 2;
        \\    _x := 3;
        \\    Ω_1 := 4;
        \\    a² := 5;
        \\    x9 := 6;
        \\    ᵃ := 7;
        \\    Δθ := 8;
        \\    print("% % % % % % % %\n", λ, 안녕, _x, Ω_1, a², x9, ᵃ, Δθ);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\`λ`, `Ω` and `Δθ` are Greek letters and `안녕` is Hangul; all are
        \\alphabetic and may start a name. `ᵃ` is a modifier letter, which is
        \\alphabetic too. `a²` ends in a superscript two, which is a digit but
        \\not a letter, so it may appear only after the first character.
    );
    subheading(9, "Marks, emoji, and superscripts");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\A combining mark belongs to neither class. An identifier written in
        \\decomposed form therefore ends at its base letter, and the mark that
        \\follows becomes a stray character.
    );
    code_block(11, "glosso", #string
        \\main :: () {
        \\    café := 1;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\With `é` written as `e` plus U+0301 that file fails, and the
        \\stray-character message quotes the combining mark, which renders on top
        \\of whatever precedes it. With `é` written precomposed as U+00E9 the
        \\same source compiles: one alphabetic code point, one identifier. No
        \\normalisation is performed in either direction, so the two spellings
        \\are simply different source text.
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\Emoji are not alphabetic, and there is no separate rule for them.
        \\`🙂 := 1;` fails with `error: '🙂'`, the ordinary stray-character
        \\message.
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\Superscripts and other non-decimal digits satisfy `isalnum` but not
        \\`isalpha`. Inside an identifier they are accepted; at the start they do
        \\not produce an identifier at all, because the scanner dispatches on the
        \\digit class and begins a number. A leading `²` therefore fails with
        \\`error: number literals must use ASCII digits`.
    );
    note(15, #string,oneline
        \\There is no diagnostic saying an identifier cannot start with a digit,
        \\because no such rule exists at the token level. `2abc := 1;` lexes as
        \\the integer `2` followed by the identifier `abc`, and the parser reads
        \\it as an expression statement and then a declaration.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    2abc := 1;
        \\    print("abc = %\n", abc);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\abc = 1
    );
    subheading(18, "The keywords");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Nineteen words are reserved. The scanner reads a whole word first and
        \\then looks it up, so a word is a keyword only when it is the entire
        \\identifier.
    );
    table_header(20, "Group   —   Keywords");
    table_row(21, "Conditions and loops   |   `if`, `ifx`, `else`, `while`, `for`, `case`");
    table_row(22, "Leaving a body   |   `return`, `break`, `continue`");
    table_row(23, "Aggregates   |   `struct`, `union`, `enum`");
    table_row(24, "Modifiers   |   `using`, `defer`, `context`, `noalias`, `where`");
    table_row(25, "Conversions   |   `cast`, `acast`");
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\Every one of them is rejected as a binding name, though the wording
        \\depends on where the parser gives up: `if := 1;` is `error: unexpected
        \\token ':='`, `struct := 1;` is `error: unexpected token 'struct'`, and
        \\`cast := 1;` is `error: cast requires a type in parentheses; use acast
        \\for contextual casts`.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Whole-word matching means a longer word that merely begins or ends with
        \\a keyword is an ordinary identifier.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    iffy := 1;
        \\    structure := 2;
        \\    forward := 3;
        \\    casing := 4;
        \\    _if := 5;
        \\    if_ := 6;
        \\    print("% % % % % %\n", iffy, structure, forward, casing, _if, if_);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\1 2 3 4 5 6
    );
    subheading(30, "Words that only look reserved");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\`typeclass`, `instance`, `distinct`, `type`, `int` and `string` are
        \\plain identifier tokens. Their meaning comes from the position they
        \\appear in, which is why each of them can also be bound as a local.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    typeclass := 1;
        \\    instance := 2;
        \\    distinct := 3;
        \\    type := 4;
        \\    int := 5;
        \\    string := 6;
        \\    print("% % % % % %\n", typeclass, instance, distinct, type, int, string);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\1 2 3 4 5 6
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\`distinct` is the clearest case: it is recognised only directly after
        \\`::` in type position, so one file can declare a nominal type and also
        \\bind a local of that name.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Handle :: distinct u32;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    distinct := 7;
        \\    h : Handle = cast(Handle) 3;
        \\    print("% %\n", distinct, cast(u32) h);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\7 3
    );
    note(37, #string,oneline
        \\`true`, `false` and `null` are not keyword tokens either, but the
        \\parser resolves those spellings to literals wherever an expression is
        \\expected. A local named `true` can be declared and then never read: the
        \\program below binds `false` to `2` and still prints the boolean, which
        \\shows as `0`.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    true := 1;
        \\    false := 2;
        \\    print("% %\n", true, false);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\1 0
    );
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\`_` on its own is a legal identifier and binds like any other name. Its
        \\separate role in patterns is described in The Pattern Vocabulary.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\Identifier comparison is exact: neither case folding nor Unicode
        \\normalisation is applied to a name before it is looked up.
    );
}

render_section_8 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A decimal integer literal is a run of ASCII digits with optional `_`
        \\separators. The separators carry no meaning; they are removed before
        \\the value is parsed.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := 12_345;
        \\    b := 1_032_000;
        \\    c := 1000000;
        \\    print("% % %\n", a, b, c);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\12345 1032000 1000000
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\A run of digits may not be empty, may not begin or end with `_`, and
        \\may not contain two adjacent separators. That rule is applied
        \\independently to four different parts of a number, each with its own
        \\message, so the diagnostic names the part that is malformed.
    );
    table_header(4, "Part   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(5, "Integer digits   |   `invalid '_' separator in number literal`");
    table_row(6, "Fraction digits   |   `invalid '_' separator in fractional literal`");
    table_row(7, "Exponent digits   |   `invalid '_' separator in scientific exponent`");
    table_row(8, "Base digits   |   `invalid '_' separator in base integer literal`");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Because the parts are checked separately, a separator next to a `.` or
        \\an `e` is legal on both sides: `1_0.2_5` and `1_000e1_0` are accepted.
        \\A leading `_` is not a number error at all, since `_1` starts an
        \\identifier; that spelling fails later with `error: unbound '_1'`.
    );
    subheading(10, "Explicit base");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\A non-decimal integer is written as a base, a `#`, and digits. The base
        \\is a decimal number between 2 and 36, and the digits are `0` to `9` and
        \\`a` to `z` read case-insensitively. Both the base and the digits may
        \\carry `_` separators.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    bin := 2#1011;
        \\    oct := 8#777;
        \\    hex := 16#ff;
        \\    hexcase := 16#Ff;
        \\    b36 := 36#Z;
        \\    wide := 16#4865_316F_774F_6C64;
        \\    print("% % % % % %\n", bin, oct, hex, hexcase, b36, wide);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\11 511 255 255 35 5216630098191412324
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\One spelling covers every radix, which is why there is no separate
        \\hexadecimal or binary prefix to remember and why base 36 costs nothing
        \\extra. The base is scanned as a number in its own right, so `1_6#ff` is
        \\the same literal as `16#ff`.
    );
    table_header(15, "Written   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(16, "`1#1`   |   `integer literal base must be between 2 and 36, got 1`");
    table_row(17, "`37#z`   |   `integer literal base must be between 2 and 36, got 37`");
    table_row(18, "`2#102`   |   `digit '2' is not valid for base 2`");
    table_row(19, "`16#`   |   `base-16 integer literal needs at least one digit`");
    table_row(20, "`16 #ff`   |   `unexpected directive 'ff'`");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\The last row is the one that surprises. The `#` is absorbed by the
        \\number scanner only when it immediately follows the digits. With a
        \\space in between, `16` is a complete integer literal and `#ff` is an
        \\ordinary directive token, which the parser then rejects by name.
    );
    subheading(22, "A based literal is a bit pattern");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\A based literal is recorded as a bit pattern rather than as a
        \\magnitude, and the checker measures it against the width of the target
        \\type instead of against that type's signed range. A mask is written for
        \\its bits, so `16#ff` is a legal `s8` and denotes `-1` there.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a : s8  = -128;
        \\    b : s8  = 16#80;
        \\    c : s8  = 16#ff;
        \\    d : u8  = 16#ff;
        \\    e : s64 = 16#8000_0000_0000_0000;
        \\    f : u64 = 18_446_744_073_709_551_615;
        \\    g : s16 = 16#ffff;
        \\    print("% % % % % % %\n", a, b, c, d, e, f, g);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\-128 -128 -1 255 -9223372036854775808 18446744073709551615 -1
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The relaxation is about width, not about range: a pattern wider than
        \\the target is still rejected. `a : s8 = 16#1ff;` gives `error: 511 does
        \\not fit s8, whose range is -128..=127` with the note `a based literal
        \\is a bit pattern, so it may use every bit of s8 - this one is wider
        \\than that`. A decimal `255` at `s8` is rejected as well, since it never
        \\had the exemption.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A leading `-` withdraws the exemption, because writing a sign says the
        \\literal is meant as a magnitude again. `a : s8 = -16#ff;` gives
        \\`error: -255 does not fit s8, whose range is -128..=127`, while
        \\`a : s8 = -16#7f;` is the ordinary `-127`.
    );
    subheading(28, "The 128-bit cap");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\Magnitudes are parsed into 128 bits. Everything that fits is exact, in
        \\both the decimal and the based form, and anything wider is a lexical
        \\error rather than a silent truncation. An earlier compiler saturated
        \\over-wide literals and produced wrong constants without saying so.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    smax : s128 = 170_141_183_460_469_231_731_687_303_715_884_105_727;
        \\    smin : s128 = -170_141_183_460_469_231_731_687_303_715_884_105_728;
        \\    umax : u128 = 340_282_366_920_938_463_463_374_607_431_768_211_455;
        \\    bits : u128 = 16#ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff;
        \\    same : s128 = 16#ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff;
        \\    print("%\n%\n%\n%\n%\n", smax, smin, umax, bits, same);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\170141183460469231731687303715884105727
        \\-170141183460469231731687303715884105728
        \\340282366920938463463374607431768211455
        \\340282366920938463463374607431768211455
        \\-1
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\One digit more and the file stops lexing. A decimal magnitude gives
        \\`error: integer literal '340282366920938463463374607431768211456' does
        \\not fit in 128 bits`, quoting the digits with the separators removed; a
        \\based one gives `error: base-16 integer literal does not fit in 128
        \\bits`.
    );
    note(33, #string,oneline
        \\The cap belongs to the literal, not to the declaration. An annotation
        \\is still needed to reach a 128-bit type, because an unannotated literal
        \\defaults to `int`: binding the `s128` maximum with `:=` gives
        \\`error: 170141183460469231731687303715884105727 does not fit int, whose
        \\range is -9223372036854775808..=9223372036854775807`. Defaulting is
        \\covered in Compile-Time Numbers and Literal Defaulting.
    );
    subheading(34, "Rejected spellings");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\The C prefixes are not merely unsupported; they are detected and named,
        \\so a constant copied out of a C header fails with an instruction rather
        \\than with a confusing split into a zero and an identifier.
    );
    table_header(36, "Written   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(37, "`0xff`   |   `legacy '0x' integer literals are not supported; use '16#digits'`");
    table_row(38, "`0B10`   |   `legacy '0b' integer literals are not supported; use '2#digits'`");
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\Both letter cases are caught, for both prefixes. There is no `0o` case,
        \\so an octal constant in the C spelling does split: `0o7` lexes as the
        \\integer `0` followed by the identifier `o7`, and fails with
        \\`error: unbound 'o7'`.
    );
}

render_section_9 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A number token becomes a float when either of two shapes appears, and
        \\one literal may use both at once.
    );
    bullet(1, #string,oneline
        \\A `.` followed by at least one digit, which opens the fraction:
        \\`1.25`, `1_234.333_333`.
    );
    bullet(2, #string,oneline
        \\An `e` or `E`, optionally followed by `+` or `-`, followed by at least
        \\one digit: `1e3`, `1E10`, `1e+2`, `1.2e-3`.
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The exponent is looked for whether or not a fraction was seen, so an
        \\integer-looking literal with an exponent is still a float. `_`
        \\separators are allowed in the integer part, the fraction and the
        \\exponent digits, under the same rules and with the same per-part
        \\diagnostics given in Integer Literals.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := 1.25;
        \\    b := 1_234.333_333;
        \\    c := 1e3;
        \\    d := 1.2e-3;
        \\    e := 1E10;
        \\    f := 1e+2;
        \\    g := 2.5e-3;
        \\    print("% % % % % % %\n", a, b, c, d, e, f, g);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(5, "text", #string
        \\1.25 1234.333333 1000 0.0012 10000000000 100 0.0025
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\Anything outside those two shapes is not a float literal. The four
        \\spellings below all look like numbers and none of them is one.
    );
    table_header(7, "Written   —   Result");
    table_row(8, "`.5`   |   A bare `.`, then `5`: `error: unexpected token '5'`");
    table_row(9, "`1.`   |   `1`, then a bare `.`: `error: reserved operator token '.' (operator '.' cannot be overloaded)`");
    table_row(10, "`1.e3`   |   `1`, then `.`, then the name `e3`: `error: member of non-struct`");
    table_row(11, "`1.5e`   |   An exponent with no digits: `error: scientific number literal needs exponent digits after 'e' or 'E'`");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\Requiring a digit on each side of the `.` is what keeps a dot next to a
        \\number unambiguous. `.` is member access, `..` is a range and `.*` is a
        \\dereference, so a fraction allowed to start or end with nothing would
        \\collide with all three.
    );
    subheading(13, "Every float literal is an f64 at lex time");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The scanner strips the separators and parses the remaining text once,
        \\at double precision. The token carries a single `f64` value, and a
        \\literal with no annotation defaults to `f64`: `x := 1.5;` followed by
        \\`y : string = x;` reports `error: got f64, expected string`.
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\A wider annotation changes where the value lands, not how it was read.
        \\An `f80` or `f128` literal has already been rounded to double precision
        \\by the time the type is known, so digits beyond roughly the seventeenth
        \\are gone before the annotation is ever consulted.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a : f80  = 1.000_000_000_000_000_000_1;
        \\    b : f80  = 1.0;
        \\    c : f128 = 1.000_000_000_000_000_000_1;
        \\    d : f128 = 1.0;
        \\    print("f80  literal == 1.0: %\n", a == b);
        \\    print("f128 literal == 1.0: %\n", c == d);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\f80  literal == 1.0: 1
        \\f128 literal == 1.0: 1
    );
    note(18, #string,oneline
        \\Both comparisons are true. `f80` and `f128` are real storage widths
        \\with real arithmetic; only the literal is limited, and a constant more
        \\precise than a double cannot be written down as one token.
    );
    subheading(19, "Out-of-range literals");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\There is no fitting check for floats. A literal too large for its type
        \\becomes an infinity and one too small becomes zero, in both cases
        \\silently. The integer diagnostic `does not fit` has no float
        \\counterpart.
    );
    table_header(21, "Written   —   Stored");
    table_row(22, "`x : f64 = 1e400;`   |   Positive infinity");
    table_row(23, "`x : f64 = 1e-400;`   |   Zero");
    table_row(24, "`x : f16 = 100000.0;`   |   Positive infinity, since `f16` stops at 65504");
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    big  : f64 = 1e400;
        \\    tiny : f64 = 1e-400;
        \\    over : f16 = 100000.0;
        \\    print("% % %\n", big > 1.797_693_134_862_315_7e308, tiny == 0.0, cast(f64) over > 1000000.0);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\1 1 1
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\The three comparisons confirm the three rows: `big` exceeds the largest
        \\finite `f64`, `tiny` has collapsed to zero, and `over` has left the
        \\`f16` range entirely.
    );
    note(28, #string,oneline
        \\Those values do not print usefully on every target. Where the host C
        \\runtime formatter is not used, `print` renders a float as an integer
        \\part plus six fraction digits, so an infinity or any magnitude past the
        \\64-bit range prints as a fixed piece of nonsense: `print("%\n",
        \\6.02e23);` gives `9223372036854775809`. Formatting is covered in
        \\`Show`, `FromString`, and `print` Formatting.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\A float literal never reaches an integer target. `x : s64 = 1.5;` is
        \\`error: cannot implicitly convert comptime_float to int`, and
        \\`x : u8 = 1.0;` is `error: cannot implicitly convert comptime_float to
        \\u8` even though the value is whole. That rule and its escape hatches
        \\are in Conversions: What Is Implicit.
    );
}

render_section_10 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A string literal is written between double quotes. The scanner walks
        \\forward to the closing quote, stepping two characters over any
        \\backslash pair so that an escaped quote does not end the literal, and
        \\stores the raw source text found between the quotes.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Decoding is deferred. The stored text still holds the backslashes as
        \\written, and escapes become characters later, when the literal turns
        \\into a value. One consequence is worth stating up front: no escape
        \\sequence is ever a lexical error, because the scanner never inspects
        \\the character after a backslash.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    controls := "\a\b\f\n\r\t\v\0";
        \\    print("count %\n", controls.count);
        \\    print("% % % % % % % %\n",
        \\        controls[0], controls[1], controls[2], controls[3],
        \\        controls[4], controls[5], controls[6], controls[7]);
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\\ \" \' \? \x41");
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\q\e\s");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\count 8
        \\7 8 12 10 13 9 11 0
        \\[\ " ' ? A]
        \\[qes]
    );
    subheading(4, "The escape table");
    table_header(5, "Escape   —   Result");
    table_row(6, #string,oneline
        \\`\a`   |   U+0007, alert
    );
    table_row(7, #string,oneline
        \\`\b`   |   U+0008, backspace
    );
    table_row(8, #string,oneline
        \\`\f`   |   U+000C, form feed
    );
    table_row(9, #string,oneline
        \\`\n`   |   U+000A, line feed
    );
    table_row(10, #string,oneline
        \\`\r`   |   U+000D, carriage return
    );
    table_row(11, #string,oneline
        \\`\t`   |   U+0009, tab
    );
    table_row(12, #string,oneline
        \\`\v`   |   U+000B, vertical tab
    );
    table_row(13, #string,oneline
        \\`\0`   |   U+0000; it does not terminate the string
    );
    table_row(14, #string,oneline
        \\`\\`   |   One backslash
    );
    table_row(15, #string,oneline
        \\`\"`   |   A double quote
    );
    table_row(16, #string,oneline
        \\`\'`   |   An apostrophe
    );
    table_row(17, #string,oneline
        \\`\?`   |   A question mark
    );
    table_row(18, #string,oneline
        \\`\xHH`   |   The code point U+00HH; exactly two hexadecimal digits
    );
    table_row(19, #string,oneline
        \\`\%`   |   Nothing; the backslash and the per-cent sign both survive
    );
    table_row(20, #string,oneline
        \\Anything else   |   The backslash is dropped and the character kept
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\The last row is why a typo is silent. `"\q\e\s"` decodes to `qes`, and
        \\a malformed hexadecimal escape degrades the same way: `"\xZZ"` is `xZZ`
        \\because `Z` is not a hexadecimal digit, and `"\xA"` is `xA` because one
        \\digit is not two.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\q\e\s");
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\xZZ");
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\xA");
        \\    print("[%]\n", "\x41\x42");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\[qes]
        \\[xZZ]
        \\[xA]
        \\[AB]
    );
    subheading(24, #string,oneline
        \\`\xHH` writes a code point, not a byte
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The two digits name a code point, not a storage byte. The character
        \\they select is then stored in the string's UTF-8 encoding, so an escape
        \\above `\x7f` occupies two bytes and `.count`, which counts bytes,
        \\reports two.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    ascii := "\x41\x7f";
        \\    high  := "\xff";
        \\    print("ascii count % bytes % %\n", ascii.count, ascii[0], ascii[1]);
        \\    print("high  count % bytes % %\n", high.count, high[0], high[1]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\ascii count 2 bytes 65 127
        \\high  count 2 bytes 195 191
    );
    note(28, #string,oneline
        \\`\xff` is U+00FF encoded as the two bytes 195 and 191, not the single
        \\byte 255. A string literal always produces valid UTF-8, so there is no
        \\literal spelling that yields an arbitrary byte; build such data as an
        \\array of `u8` instead.
    );
    subheading(29, #string,oneline
        \\`\%` and the runtime formatter
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\`%` is the placeholder in a format string, so a literal per-cent sign
        \\written in a format is counted as a placeholder and the call fails.
    );
    code_block(31, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("50% off\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(32, "text", #string
        \\error: print: format has 1 '%' placeholder(s) but 0 argument(s) given
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\`\%` is the answer, and it is the one escape the decoder deliberately
        \\leaves alone: both characters survive into the stored value. What turns
        \\them into a single `%` is the runtime formatter, which recognises `\%`
        \\while scanning the format and emits a per-cent sign without consuming
        \\an argument.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("100\% done\n");
        \\    print("[%]\n", "pct=\%");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\100% done
        \\[pct=\%]
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The second line shows the division of labour. That `\%` sits in an
        \\argument rather than in the format, nothing scans it as a format, and
        \\both characters print.
    );
    subheading(37, "Line breaks and termination");
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\A literal may span physical lines. The newline is part of the text, and
        \\the line counter is corrected afterwards so that later diagnostics
        \\still report the right line. A backslash before a newline is not a line
        \\continuation: it is consumed as an escape pair, the unknown-escape rule
        \\drops the backslash, and the newline stays in the string.
    );
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    plain := "a
        \\b";
        \\    slashed := "a\
        \\b";
        \\    print("% %\n", plain.count, slashed.count);
        \\    print("% %\n", plain[1], slashed[1]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\3 3
        \\10 10
    );
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\Both strings are three bytes long and both carry a line feed, byte 10,
        \\in the middle. A literal with no closing quote is an error rather than
        \\a run to end of file: `s := "abc;` alone on its line gives
        \\`error: unterminated string literal`, underlining from the opening
        \\quote to the end of the line.
    );
    subheading(42, "The four string types");
    paragraph(43, #string,oneline
        \\One literal syntax serves all four built-in string types; the
        \\annotation decides which is built, and whether a terminating NUL and a
        \\UTF-16 re-encoding are added. `.count` is the element count in every
        \\case and never includes a terminator.
    );
    code_block(44, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    plain : string    = "hello";
        \\    c     : cstring   = "hello";
        \\    wide  : string16  = "hello";
        \\    cwide : cstring16 = "hello";
        \\    print("% % % %\n", plain.count, c.count, wide.count, cwide.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(45, "text", #string
        \\5 5 5 5
    );
    paragraph(46, #string,oneline
        \\A `cstring` exists to be handed to C and has no `Show` instance, so it
        \\cannot be printed directly; the attempt reports error: variadic arg
        \\type `cstring` does not implement `Show` in call `print`. The types
        \\themselves are covered in Built-in Strings.
    );
}

render_section_11 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#string` builds a string literal out of a run of marked lines. Each
        \\line of the block begins with the marker `\\`, and everything after
        \\those two backslashes, to the end of that line, is captured.
        \\The directive collects every consecutive marked line and joins them
        \\into one literal.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\BANNER :: #string
        \\\\alpha
        \\\\beta
        \\;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("[%] count=%\n", BANNER, BANNER.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\[alpha
        \\beta] count=10
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The marker is a token in its own right, produced by the scanner
        \\wherever two backslashes appear. A single backslash is not a token at
        \\all: it is the stray-character error `error: '\'`. A marked line is
        \\consumed only by `#string` and by the structured form of `#asm`,
        \\described in the assembly chapter; anywhere else the parser reports the
        \\captured text as the offending token, so a stray `bare := \\line one`
        \\gives `error: unexpected token 'line one'`.
    );

    subheading(4, "What the marker captures");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Capture starts immediately after the second backslash and stops at the
        \\newline, which is not part of the text. Whitespace before the marker is
        \\ordinary whitespace and is discarded, so a block can be indented to
        \\match its surroundings without changing its value. Whitespace after the
        \\marker is text. Nothing inside the captured span is interpreted:
        \\quotation marks, backslashes, `//` and `/* */` are all ordinary
        \\characters.
    );
    bullet(6, #string,oneline
        \\Indentation written before the marker positions the block in the
        \\source and never reaches the string; indentation written after the
        \\marker does reach it.
    );
    bullet(7, "A marked line with no text after the marker contributes an empty line.");
    bullet(8, #string,oneline
        \\Comments are removed before the directive sees the token stream, so a
        \\`//` or `/* */` comment written between two marked lines does not
        \\interrupt the run: the lines on either side still join into one
        \\literal.
    );
    bullet(9, #string,oneline
        \\No escape decoding runs unless `,escape` is given, so a `\t` written
        \\after a marker is a backslash followed by the letter `t`, two
        \\characters.
    );
    code_block(10, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\INDENTED :: #string
        \\    \\    four spaces survive
        \\    \\next
        \\;
        \\
        \\VERBATIM :: #string
        \\\\he said "hi" // not a comment /* nor this */ \n \t \\
        \\;
        \\
        \\WITH_BLANK :: #string
        \\\\a
        \\\\
        \\\\b
        \\;
        \\
        \\COMMENTED :: #string
        \\\\alpha
        \\// this comment never reaches the directive
        \\\\beta
        \\;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("INDENTED   [%] count=%\n", INDENTED, INDENTED.count);
        \\    print("VERBATIM   [%] count=%\n", VERBATIM, VERBATIM.count);
        \\    print("WITH_BLANK [%] count=%\n", WITH_BLANK, WITH_BLANK.count);
        \\    print("COMMENTED  [%] count=%\n", COMMENTED, COMMENTED.count);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(11, "text", #string
        \\INDENTED   [    four spaces survive
        \\next] count=28
        \\VERBATIM   [he said "hi" // not a comment /* nor this */ \n \t \\] count=53
        \\WITH_BLANK [a
        \\
        \\b] count=4
        \\COMMENTED  [alpha
        \\beta] count=10
    );
    note(12, #string,oneline
        \\Because capture runs to the end of the line, the semicolon that ends
        \\the declaration must sit on a line of its own. `SAME :: #string \\text
        \\;` is a legal declaration whose value is the six characters `text ;`,
        \\not a two-word string.
    );

    subheading(13, "The joining rule");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The consecutive marked lines are collected in source order and joined
        \\with a single separator: a newline by default, a single space under
        \\`,oneline`. The separator goes between lines only, so a block never
        \\ends with one. At least one marked line is required; `V :: #string;`
        \\reports the error below.
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\error: #string expects one or more '\\' lines
    );
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`#string` is an expression, not only a declaration form, so a block may
        \\appear anywhere a string is expected: a local initialiser, an argument,
        \\a struct field.
    );

    subheading(17, "The two modifiers");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\Two modifiers exist and no others. They are written as comma-separated
        \\identifiers directly after the directive, may be given in either order,
        \\and may each be given at most once. `,oneline` changes the separator
        \\from a newline to a single space. `,escape` changes how the joined text
        \\is stored: without it the text is already final and backslashes stay
        \\literal, with it the ordinary escape table described in the chapter on
        \\string literals is applied to the joined text.
    );
    table_header(19, "Form   —   Separator   —   Escape decoding");
    table_row(20, "`#string`   |   newline   |   none: backslashes are literal");
    table_row(21, "`#string,oneline`   |   one space   |   none: backslashes are literal");
    table_row(22, "`#string,escape`   |   newline   |   the ordinary escape table");
    table_row(23, "`#string,escape,oneline`   |   one space   |   the ordinary escape table");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\The four forms differ on exactly two axes, so the same two marked lines
        \\produce four different strings. This program reports each result by
        \\length and by the byte codes at offsets three, four and five, which is
        \\where the two axes show up.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\RAW :: #string
        \\\\bar\t
        \\\\foo
        \\;
        \\
        \\ONELINE :: #string,oneline
        \\\\bar\t
        \\\\foo
        \\;
        \\
        \\ESCAPED :: #string,escape
        \\\\bar\t
        \\\\foo
        \\;
        \\
        \\BOTH :: #string,escape,oneline
        \\\\bar\t
        \\\\foo
        \\;
        \\
        \\report :: (name: string, value: string) {
        \\    print("% count=% bytes 3..5 = % % %\n", name, value.count,
        \\        value[3], value[4], value[5]);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    report("RAW                  ", RAW);
        \\    report("ONELINE              ", ONELINE);
        \\    report("ESCAPED              ", ESCAPED);
        \\    report("ESCAPE + ONELINE     ", BOTH);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\RAW                   count=9 bytes 3..5 = 92 116 10
        \\ONELINE               count=9 bytes 3..5 = 92 116 32
        \\ESCAPED               count=8 bytes 3..5 = 9 10 102
        \\ESCAPE + ONELINE      count=8 bytes 3..5 = 9 32 102
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Byte 3 is `92` and byte 4 is `116` in the two undecoded forms: a
        \\backslash and a `t`, nine characters in all. Under `,escape` the pair
        \\has become the single tab `9` and the string is one character shorter.
        \\Byte 5 shows the separator: `10` is the newline of the default join,
        \\`32` the space of `,oneline`.
    );
    bullet(28, #string,oneline
        \\Under `,oneline` a blank marked line still contributes a segment, so
        \\the three lines `a`, empty, `b` join as `a  b`, four characters with
        \\two spaces.
    );
    bullet(29, #string,oneline
        \\`,escape` decodes escapes written inside the captured text; it has no
        \\effect on the marker, which is consumed by the scanner before any of
        \\the text exists.
    );
    bullet(30, #string,oneline
        \\`\%` survives escape decoding as the two characters `\` and `%`, in a
        \\marked block exactly as in a quoted literal, because it is the runtime
        \\formatter and not the lexer that turns it into a per-cent sign.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\That last rule is why an undecoded block still works as a `print`
        \\format. The block below carries a literal `\%` and a `%` placeholder,
        \\and both behave as they would in a quoted format string.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\FORMAT_RAW :: #string
        \\\\raw format: 100\% and a value %
        \\;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print(FORMAT_RAW, 7);
        \\    print("\n");
        \\    inline_use := #string,oneline
        \\        \\an expression, not only a declaration,
        \\        \\joined across two marked lines
        \\    ;
        \\    print("[%]\n", inline_use);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\raw format: 100% and a value 7
        \\[an expression, not only a declaration, joined across two marked lines]
    );

    subheading(34, "Modifier errors");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\An identifier after the comma that is neither `oneline` nor `escape` is
        \\rejected by name, and so is a repeat of either one. Both diagnostics
        \\quote the modifier back.
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\V :: #string,wrapped
        \\error: unknown #string modifier 'wrapped'; expected 'oneline' or 'escape'
        \\
        \\V :: #string,oneline,oneline
        \\error: duplicate #string modifier 'oneline'
        \\
        \\V :: #string,escape,escape
        \\error: duplicate #string modifier 'escape'
    );
    note(37, #string,oneline
        \\Capture stops at the newline character, not at the line ending. In a
        \\file saved with CRLF line endings every line of a block therefore keeps
        \\a trailing carriage return. The two-line block `alpha` / `beta` has
        \\`count == 10` saved with LF and `count == 12` saved with CRLF, and
        \\nothing in the program says which one it got.
    );
}

render_section_12 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#char` writes a character as an integer literal. The scanner handles
        \\it inside the directive scan and emits an integer token, so a `#char`
        \\is not a directive the parser sees and not a value of some character
        \\type. Glosso has no character type; a `#char` is a number that happens
        \\to have been written as a glyph.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := #char "A";
        \\    l := #char "λ";
        \\    print("A      = % (%)\n", a, type_name(type_of(a)));
        \\    print("lambda = % (%)\n", l, type_name(type_of(l)));
        \\    print("A + 1  = %\n", a + 1);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\A      = 65 (u8)
        \\lambda = 955 (u32)
        \\A + 1  = 66
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The form is the directive, then optional spaces and tabs, then a quoted
        \\string holding exactly one character or one two-character escape. The
        \\separator may be empty, so `#char"A"` is the same literal as `#char
        \\"A"`. It may not contain a newline: a string on the following line is
        \\`error: #char expects a string literal`.
    );

    subheading(4, "Why it is an integer and not a character");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Indexing a `string` yields a `u8`, and byte classification is written
        \\as ordinary integer comparison. Making `#char` an integer literal is
        \\what lets those comparisons be written with glyphs instead of numbers,
        \\with no conversion and no character type to carry through the program.
        \\The arithmetic is the underlying integer arithmetic, with the same
        \\overflow rules as any other value of that width.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\is_digit_byte :: (byte: u8) -> bool {
        \\    return byte >= #char "0" && byte <= #char "9";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    text : string = "h7";
        \\    print("first is h: %\n", text[0] == #char "h");
        \\    print("second is a digit: %\n", is_digit_byte(text[1]));
        \\    print("A + 32 = %\n", #char "A" + 32);
        \\    upper := text[0] - #char "a" + #char "A";
        \\    print("upper h = %\n", upper);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\first is h: 1
        \\second is a digit: 1
        \\A + 32 = 97
        \\upper h = 72
    );

    subheading(8, "The accepted escapes");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The scanner decodes the escape itself rather than deferring to the
        \\string machinery, so `#char` has its own table. It is the C table
        \\without the numeric forms.
    );
    table_header(10, "Escape   —   Value   —   Character");
    table_row(11, "`\\a`   |   7   |   alert");
    table_row(12, "`\\b`   |   8   |   backspace");
    table_row(13, "`\\f`   |   12   |   form feed");
    table_row(14, "`\\n`   |   10   |   line feed");
    table_row(15, "`\\r`   |   13   |   carriage return");
    table_row(16, "`\\t`   |   9   |   tab");
    table_row(17, "`\\v`   |   11   |   vertical tab");
    table_row(18, "`\\0`   |   0   |   NUL");
    table_row(19, "`\\\\`   |   92   |   backslash");
    table_row(20, "`\\\"`   |   34   |   quotation mark");
    table_row(21, "`\\'`   |   39   |   apostrophe");
    table_row(22, "`\\?`   |   63   |   question mark");
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("a=% b=% f=% n=% r=% t=% v=%\n",
        \\        #char "\a", #char "\b", #char "\f", #char "\n",
        \\        #char "\r", #char "\t", #char "\v");
        \\    print("0=% backslash=% quote=% apostrophe=% question=%\n",
        \\        #char "\0", #char "\\", #char "\"", #char "\'", #char "\?");
        \\    print("unknown escape = %\n", #char "\q");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\a=7 b=8 f=12 n=10 r=13 t=9 v=11
        \\0=0 backslash=92 quote=34 apostrophe=39 question=63
        \\unknown escape = 113
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\An escape outside the table yields the escaped character itself, which
        \\is why `#char "\q"` is `113`, the code of `q`. The escaped character is
        \\taken as a single byte, so escaping a character outside ASCII truncates
        \\it: `#char "\λ"` is `187`, the low byte of `955`.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\There is no `\xHH` form, and no `\u{...}` form either. The scan accepts
        \\one character or one backslash-plus-character pair and nothing longer,
        \\so a numeric escape trips the length check.
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\main :: () { c := #char "\x41"; }
        \\error: #char string literal contains more than two characters
        \\
        \\main :: () { c := #char ""; }
        \\error: #char expects one character
        \\
        \\main :: () { c := #char 5; }
        \\error: #char expects a string literal
        \\
        \\main :: () { c := #char "a; }
        \\error: unterminated #char string literal
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A code point above ASCII is written directly instead: `#char "λ"` is
        \\`955`, the whole code point, because the file is decoded to code points
        \\before the scan and the literal takes one of them.
    );

    subheading(29, "The synthesised type");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\Left alone, a `#char` synthesises as `u8` when its code point is at
        \\most `0x7f` and as `u32` otherwise. The split is deliberate: an ASCII
        \\literal is almost always about to meet a string byte, and a non-ASCII
        \\one cannot fit in a byte at all. Given an expected type, the literal
        \\accepts only the types that can hold what was written.
    );
    table_header(31, "Code point   —   Default type   —   Types it accepts");
    table_row(32, "at most `0x7f`   |   `u8`   |   `u8`, `s8`, `u32`");
    table_row(33, "above `0x7f`   |   `u32`   |   `u32`");
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\main :: () { c : s64 = #char "A"; }
        \\error: ASCII #char literal can infer only as u8, s8, or u32, not int
        \\
        \\main :: () { c : u16 = #char "A"; }
        \\error: ASCII #char literal can infer only as u8, s8, or u32, not u16
        \\
        \\main :: () { c : u8 = #char "λ"; }
        \\error: non-ASCII #char literal can infer only as u32, not u8
    );
    note(35, #string,oneline
        \\The list is closed, so `u16` is refused even though it is wide enough.
        \\A `#char` at any other integer type has to go through a cast, and a
        \\cast accepts it because at that point it is a number like any other:
        \\`cast(u16) #char "A"` is `65`.
    );
}

render_section_13 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\The scanner produces tokens of a fixed set of kinds. Punctuation and
        \\operators are drawn from the same eighteen characters, and which one a
        \\run of those characters becomes is decided by a table of special cases
        \\first and a maximal-munch rule afterwards. Every spelling in the
        \\punctuation table below is a token kind of its own, which is why none
        \\of them can be redeclared as an operator.
    );

    subheading(1, "The punctuation table");
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\The middle column names the token kind the scanner emits. Two of these
        \\spellings, `.*` and `?`, are emitted as operator tokens rather than
        \\dedicated kinds, but they are punctuation in practice and are listed
        \\here with the rest.
    );
    table_header(3, "Spelling   —   Token   —   Meaning");
    table_row(4, "`;`   |   Semicolon   |   statement terminator");
    table_row(5, "`,`   |   Comma   |   separator in lists and literals");
    table_row(6, "`,,`   |   CommaComma   |   nonlocal label, as in `main,,'entry`");
    table_row(7, "`.`   |   Dot   |   member access");
    table_row(8, "`.{` `.[`   |   DotBrace, DotBracket   |   aggregate literal, array literal");
    table_row(9, "`.*`   |   Op   |   pointer dereference, written after the pointer");
    table_row(10, "`..` `..=`   |   Range, RangeInclusive   |   exclusive and inclusive ranges");
    table_row(11, "`...`   |   Ellipsis   |   variadic parameter and the rest pattern");
    table_row(12, "`:`   |   Colon   |   type annotation");
    table_row(13, "`::`   |   ColonColon   |   constant binding");
    table_row(14, "`:=`   |   ColonEqual   |   variable binding");
    table_row(15, "`::=`   |   ColonColonEqual   |   compile-time binding");
    table_row(16, "`=`   |   Equal   |   assignment");
    table_row(17, "`->`   |   Arrow   |   return type");
    table_row(18, "`$`   |   Dollar   |   generic parameter introducer");
    table_row(19, "`?`   |   Op   |   optional type former and the propagation suffix");
    table_row(20, "`@`   |   Op   |   pattern binder, as in `whole @ subpattern`");
    table_row(21, "`(` `)` `{` `}` `[` `]`   |   LParen through RBracket   |   grouping, blocks, brackets");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Two absences are worth naming. `.(`, which opens a tuple literal, is
        \\not one token: it is `Dot` followed by `LParen`. And `x : := e` is not
        \\a third form; the scanner emits `Colon` and then `ColonEqual`, and the
        \\parser reads that pair as `::=`. The remaining token kinds carry text
        \\rather than punctuation: `Ident`, `Int`, `Float`, `Str`, `MlStr`,
        \\`Directive`, `Op`, `OpName`, `Backtick`, `DoubleBacktick` and `Label`,
        \\each covered by the chapter that owns its construct.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Point :: struct { x: int; y: int; }
        \\
        \\sum_all :: (values: ...int) -> int {
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    for v : values total += v;
        \\    return total;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    K :: 10;                          // ::   constant binding
        \\    n := 3;                           // :=   variable binding
        \\    m ::= 6 * 7;                      // ::=  compile-time binding
        \\    q : int = 5;                      // :    type annotation
        \\    q = 6;                            // =    assignment
        \\    p : Point = .{ .x = 1, .y = 2 };  // .{   aggregate literal
        \\    a : [5]int = .[10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
        \\    t := .(1, "two");                 // .(   Dot then LParen
        \\    print("K=% n=% m=% q=% p=% a=% t=%\n", K, n, m, q, p.x, a[2], t.0);
        \\    print("full=% tail=% head=% mid=%\n",
        \\        a[..].count, a[2..].count, a[..2].count, a[1..=3].count);
        \\    print("varargs=%\n", sum_all(1, 2, 3));
        \\    r := *p;
        \\    print("deref x=%\n", r.*.x);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\K=10 n=3 m=42 q=6 p=1 a=30 t=1
        \\full=5 tail=3 head=2 mid=3
        \\varargs=6
        \\deref x=1
    );

    subheading(25, "The operator character set");
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\Exactly eighteen ASCII characters may appear in an operator token. Any
        \\run of them that is not caught by one of the special cases below is
        \\scanned as a single operator token.
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\+  -  *  /  <  >  !  @  %  ^  &  |  ~  ?  .  ,  :  =
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\What is absent matters as much as what is present. `$`, `#`, `\`, `'`,
        \\backtick, `"`, `;` and the six bracket characters are not operator
        \\characters, so they always end an operator run. That is why `x: []int`
        \\and `Err![]T` lex the way they read, and why an operator whose name
        \\contains a bracket can only be written in the quoted form described in
        \\the chapter on reserved operators.
    );

    subheading(29, "Maximal munch and its exceptions");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The scanner takes the longest run of operator characters it can, which
        \\it must: a library may introduce any spelling, so the scanner cannot
        \\know that `+|` is one operator and `+` `|` two without preferring the
        \\longer read. Five characters carry extra rules on top of that. `?`
        \\never continues an operator, so `*?T` is three tokens and a propagation
        \\suffix composes with whatever follows it. A lone `@` is taken by
        \\itself, but only when no operator character follows, which keeps `@=`
        \\and `@>` available as user spellings. After `:` the scanner checks the
        \\fixed forms `::=`, `::` and `:=` first, then falls into the general
        \\scan unless the next character is `*`. After `=` it falls into the
        \\general scan whenever another operator character follows. A trailing
        \\`.` is handed back when the character after it could start a
        \\non-operator token, which is what makes `p.*.b` parse; if a token still
        \\ends in `.` it is `error: operator token cannot end with '.'`.
    );
    table_header(31, "Written   —   Lexes as   —   Result");
    table_row(32, "`a = -1`   |   `=` `-` `1`   |   assignment of a negated literal");
    table_row(33, "`a =-1`   |   `=-`   |   `error: unexpected token '=-'`");
    table_row(34, "`1 ++2`   |   `++`   |   `error: unexpected token '++'`");
    table_row(35, "`o: ?int`   |   `:` then `?int`   |   an optional annotation");
    table_row(36, "`o:?int`   |   `:` alone   |   `error: reserved operator token ':'`");
    table_row(37, "`p:*int`   |   `:` `*`   |   works, by the `*` exception");
    table_row(38, "`Err! *int`   |   `!` `*`   |   a result of a pointer");
    table_row(39, "`Err!*int`   |   `!*`   |   `error: unexpected token '!*'`");
    table_row(40, "`2 * p.*.b`   |   `*` `.` `*` `.` `b`   |   works, by the trailing-dot rule");
    table_row(41, "`2 *. 3`   |   `*.`   |   `error: operator token cannot end with '.'`");
    note(42, #string,oneline
        \\Whitespace between two operator characters is load-bearing. The two
        \\pairs a program is most likely to write by accident are `x:?T`, which
        \\loses the `?` and reports a reserved `:`, and `E!*T`, which glues `!`
        \\and `*` into one unknown operator. Both read perfectly well and both
        \\fail; a space fixes each.
    );
}

render_section_14 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Fourteen operator spellings are reserved. The check lives in the
        \\scanner and fires on the token, not on the declaration, so it catches a
        \\reserved spelling written as a quoted operator name and a reserved
        \\spelling that reaches operator position by accident with the same
        \\message. The message always names the operator twice, once for the
        \\token and once for the rule.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\X :: '=';
        \\main :: () {}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\error: reserved operator token '=' (operator '=' cannot be overloaded)
    );

    subheading(3, "The reserved spellings");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Each of these carries syntax the parser must be able to recognise
        \\without consulting any operator table, which is what makes it
        \\unavailable for overloading.
    );
    table_header(5, "Spelling   —   What it is");
    table_row(6, "`=`   |   assignment");
    table_row(7, "`.`   |   member access");
    table_row(8, "`,`   |   list separator");
    table_row(9, "`,,`   |   nonlocal label constructor");
    table_row(10, "`:=`   |   variable binding");
    table_row(11, "`::`   |   constant binding");
    table_row(12, "`:`   |   type annotation");
    table_row(13, "`->`   |   return-type arrow");
    table_row(14, "`::=`   |   compile-time binding");
    table_row(15, "`.*`   |   pointer dereference");
    table_row(16, "`...`   |   varargs and the rest pattern");
    table_row(17, "`@`   |   pattern binder");
    table_row(18, "backtick   |   hygiene escape");
    table_row(19, "double backtick   |   code splice");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\Because the check is on the token, a mistyped expression can produce
        \\it. `x := 1.;` is not a float, so the trailing `.` reaches the operator
        \\scanner and reports `error: reserved operator token '.' (operator '.'
        \\cannot be overloaded)`. So does `. {`, written with a space, which is
        \\why `.{` has to be spelled without one.
    );

    subheading(21, "Why `..`, `..=` and `?` are not on the list");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\All three have dedicated token kinds and dedicated parser handling, so
        \\they look like candidates for the list. They are absent on purpose. A
        \\range and a propagation are both defined by typeclasses in the library
        \\rather than built into the compiler, and a user type joins in by
        \\writing an instance. Reserving the spellings would make that
        \\impossible.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\// std/Prelude/Primitive_Operators.glo
        \\RangeExclusiveOp :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    '..' :: (start: L, end: R) -> Output #operator(left, 2);
        \\}
        \\
        \\// std/Typeclass/Try.glo
        \\'?' :: (value: Carrier) -> Try_Branch(Output, Residual) #operator(suffix) {
        \\    return branch(value);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\The proof is in the diagnostic a competing declaration gets. Declaring
        \\`'..'` in a new typeclass is not rejected as reserved; it is rejected
        \\because the spelling already belongs to a class, which is the ordinary
        \\rule for every operator.
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\MyRange :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    '..' :: (a: L, b: R) -> Output #operator(left, 2);
        \\}
        \\error: operator '..' is already owned by typeclass 'RangeExclusiveOp'
    );

    subheading(26, "The quoted operator-name form");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\An operator is named by writing its characters between single quotes.
        \\After a quote the scanner looks ahead: if what follows is a letter or
        \\`_` and the run is not closed by a second quote, the token is a label
        \\such as `'again`; otherwise it scans to the next quote and produces an
        \\operator name. So `'+'` names an operator and `'done` declares a label,
        \\with no ambiguity between them.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\The text between the quotes is taken as written. The scanner applies
        \\only the reserved-spelling check and the trailing-dot check, so a name
        \\may contain characters that could never be lexed as an operator run,
        \\including letters and brackets. The declaration then succeeds even when
        \\the name can never be written at a use site.
    );
    bullet(29, #string,oneline
        \\A letters-only name such as `'plus'` declares cleanly, but at a use
        \\site the letters lex as an identifier: `2 plus 3` is `error: unexpected
        \\token 'plus'`.
    );
    bullet(30, #string,oneline
        \\`''` is a legal empty name, and using it produces `error: unbound ''`.
    );
    bullet(31, #string,oneline
        \\`'[]'` and `'[]='` are the indexing and index-assignment operators.
        \\They are the only names allowed to contain a bracket; any other gives
        \\`error: operator '<[>' cannot contain '[' or ']'` with the offending
        \\name quoted back.
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Beyond the spelling, the checker constrains what an operator
        \\declaration may say. These rules are enforced when the declaration is
        \\checked, not when it is scanned.
    );
    table_header(33, "Rule   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(34, "one to three parameters   |   `operator functions need one, two, or three parameters`");
    table_row(35, "must belong to a typeclass   |   `operator '<~>' must be declared by a typeclass and implemented by its instances`");
    table_row(36, "one owning class per spelling   |   `operator '+' is already owned by typeclass 'Add'`");
    table_row(37, "`'[]'` takes exactly two   |   `operator '[]' must have arity 2`");
    table_row(38, "an all-stars name is never prefix   |   `operator '**' cannot be overloaded as a prefix operator`");
    note(39, #string,oneline
        \\The all-stars rule exists because `*`, `**`, `***` and so on are the
        \\pointer sigils in type position. A prefix instance of one of them would
        \\make `**T` ambiguous between a pointer to a pointer and an application
        \\of a user operator, so the prefix fixity is refused while the binary
        \\one stays available.
    );
}

render_section_15 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Before a file is parsed, its whole token vector is walked once by the
        \\prescan, which looks for operator declarations and records their
        \\fixity, precedence and associativity. Only then does parsing begin.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The prescan exists because Glosso lets a library introduce operator
        \\spellings and precedences. Parsing cannot decide how `2 * 3 %% 4`
        \\groups, or even whether `%%` is an operator at all, until every
        \\`#operator` and `#precedence` declaration visible to the file has been
        \\read. A single-pass parser would have to see the declaration first;
        \\the prescan removes that ordering requirement by reading the
        \\declarations out of the token stream before any of them is parsed.
    );

    subheading(2, "What the prescan recognises");
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The pattern is narrow and purely lexical: an operator name, then `::`,
        \\then an opening parenthesis. From the matching close parenthesis the
        \\scan runs forward to the first `{` or `;`, which is to say across the
        \\signature and its directives and no further, looking for `#operator` or
        \\`#precedence`.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\'%%' :: (a: L, b: R) -> Output #precedence(left, 19);
        \\'~~' :: (value: T) -> Output #operator(prefix);
        \\'!!' :: (value: T) -> Output #operator(suffix);
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\`#operator(mode)` and `#operator(mode, prec)` register a fixity and,
        \\where the mode carries one, a precedence. `#precedence(mode, prec)`
        \\registers precedence and associativity only, which is what a typeclass
        \\method declaration usually wants.
    );
    table_header(6, "Mode   —   Registers   —   Meaning");
    table_row(7, "`left`   |   precedence, left associativity   |   ordinary binary operator");
    table_row(8, "`right`   |   precedence, right associativity   |   right-grouping binary operator");
    table_row(9, "`assign`   |   precedence, right associativity   |   assignment-shaped operator");
    table_row(10, "`prefix`   |   prefix fixity   |   written before its operand");
    table_row(11, "`suffix`   |   suffix fixity   |   written after its operand");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\A prefix or suffix mode carries no precedence, and `#precedence` with
        \\one is rejected: `#precedence(prefix)` is `error: #precedence expects
        \\left, right, or assign`. A name made only of asterisks is never
        \\registered as prefix, because those spellings are the pointer sigils.
    );

    subheading(13, "An operator may be used before it is declared");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\That is the whole visible consequence. The declaration below sits after
        \\`main`, and `main` uses the operator anyway, at the precedence the
        \\later declaration asks for.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("3 \%\% 4       = %\n", 3 %% 4);
        \\    print("2 * 3 \%\% 4   = %\n", 2 * 3 %% 4);
        \\}
        \\
        \\Mod :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    '%%' :: (a: L, b: R) -> Output #precedence(left, 19);
        \\}
        \\
        \\(int, int) :: instance Mod {
        \\    Output :: int;
        \\    '%%' :: (a: int, b: int) -> Output { return a * 10 + b; }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\3 %% 4       = 34
        \\2 * 3 %% 4   = 68
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Precedence 19 binds tighter than `*` at 18, so the second line is
        \\`2 * (3 %% 4)`, which is `2 * 34`. Changing the one number in the
        \\declaration changes how the expression above it groups: at 17 the
        \\operator binds looser than `*` and the same source reads
        \\`(2 * 3) %% 4`.
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\3 %% 4       = 34
        \\2 * 3 %% 4   = 64
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\An operator with no `#operator` and no `#precedence` is registered by
        \\nothing, so its spelling never becomes an operator and the use site
        \\fails as an unknown token. The declaration is reported too, with the
        \\directive it is missing.
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\error: unexpected token '%%'
        \\error: binary operator '%%' needs #operator(left|right|assign, n)
    );
    note(21, #string,oneline
        \\The precedence must be a written integer literal. The prescan reads it
        \\straight out of the token stream, before any name means anything, so a
        \\named constant cannot be used: `#precedence(left, P)` with `P :: 19;`
        \\is `error: unexpected token 'P'`, reported at the constant, and the
        \\operator is never registered.
    );

    subheading(22, "The operator table is per file");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Each source file is prescanned on its own, and the table it gets is the
        \\Prelude's operators plus whatever that file itself declares. Nothing
        \\else reaches it. A file loaded with `#load` is prescanned separately
        \\from the file that loads it, so an operator declared in the loaded file
        \\works inside that file and is an unknown token in the other.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\// Mod2.glo: combine uses %% on its first line, before the declaration
        \\combine :: (a: int, b: int) -> int { return a %% b; }
        \\
        \\Mod :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    '%%' :: (a: L, b: R) -> Output #precedence(left, 19);
        \\}
        \\
        \\(int, int) :: instance Mod {
        \\    Output :: int;
        \\    '%%' :: (a: int, b: int) -> Output { return a * 10 + b; }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#load "Mod2.glo";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("combine(3, 4) = %\n", combine(3, 4));   // fine
        \\    print("%\n", 3 %% 4);                          // not fine
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\error: unexpected token '%%'
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Removing the second `print` makes the program compile and print
        \\`combine(3, 4) = 34`: the operator is real, its instance is real, and
        \\the function that uses it is callable. Only the spelling failed to
        \\cross the file boundary.
    );
    bullet(28, #string,oneline
        \\`#import` behaves the same way. A module that introduces a new operator
        \\spelling can use it throughout its own files, but the importing file
        \\still cannot write it.
    );
    bullet(29, #string,oneline
        \\The Prelude is prescanned before anything else and its table seeds
        \\every file, which is why the arithmetic, comparison, bitwise and range
        \\spellings are available with no import and no declaration.
    );
    bullet(30, #string,oneline
        \\Within one standard module the files share an accumulating table, so a
        \\spelling declared in one file of a module is usable in the module's
        \\other files.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\The practical rule that falls out of this is short: every operator a
        \\file can write is either one the Prelude declares or one declared in
        \\that same file, and within the file its position does not matter.
    );
}
